<div class="pd-treemap-documentation">

	<section id="overview">
		<h2 class="doc-section">Overview</h2>
		<p>
			<code>PDTreeMap</code> renders a hierarchy as an area-proportional tree map, in the style of
			WizTree or WinDirStat. The area of every rectangle is proportional to a size metric you supply,
			so whatever is consuming the most of a resource is the largest thing on screen.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>Layout is computed in C# by <code>TreeMapLayoutEngine</code> using the squarified algorithm, and rendered as inline SVG. There is no charting dependency.</li>
			<li>Children are drawn nested inside their parent, down to <code>MaxRenderDepth</code>.</li>
			<li>Clicking a branch zooms into it; a breadcrumb takes you back out.</li>
			<li>Four colour modes, plus an explicit <code>ColourSelector</code> that overrides them all.</li>
			<li>Keyboard accessible: arrow keys move, <kbd>Enter</kbd> zooms in, <kbd>Backspace</kbd> or <kbd>Esc</kbd> zooms out.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			The component renders the tree map <em>only</em>. To reproduce the full WizTree experience, pair it
			with a <code>PDTable</code> bound to the same data and cross-select using <code>SelectionChanged</code>
			and <code>ZoomRoot</code>. Keeping the grid out of this component avoids duplicating
			<code>PDTable</code> and avoids baking a layout opinion into the library.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="required">
		<h2 class="doc-section">The three things you must supply</h2>
		<p>
			Everything else has a sensible default. <code>PDTreeMap</code> is generic over your own type, so
			there is no node model to convert into.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><code>Root</code> — the top of your hierarchy. The root itself is not drawn; its children fill the area.</li>
			<li><code>ChildrenSelector</code> — returns an item's children, or <code>null</code> for a leaf.</li>
			<li><code>SizeSelector</code> — returns an item's size. This determines area.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section id="size-mode">
		<h2 class="doc-section">SizeMode — read this one before you use it</h2>
		<p>
			<code>SizeMode</code> is the parameter most likely to give you a wrong-looking map, because the
			right answer depends entirely on where your data came from.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<code>TreeMapSizeMode.Aggregate</code> (the default) treats a branch's size as its own size
				<em>plus every descendant</em>. This is what you want for a file system, where a directory
				listing reports zero bytes for the directory itself and the size only becomes meaningful once
				its contents are summed.
			</li>
			<li>
				<code>TreeMapSizeMode.Explicit</code> takes a branch's size as exactly what
				<code>SizeSelector</code> returned, ignoring descendants. This is what you want for a database
				size query, where each row already reports a subtree total. Leaving this on
				<code>Aggregate</code> would count the same bytes twice at every level.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p class="text-muted small">
			Either way, only the proportions between siblings matter at each level, because the children of a
			node always fill that node's rectangle.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="depth">
		<h2 class="doc-section">Depth and totals</h2>
		<p>
			<code>MaxRenderDepth</code> caps how many levels are drawn nested at once. An item at the cut that
			still has children is drawn as a single rectangle, marked with a dashed border, and its
			<code>Size</code> still accounts for its <em>whole</em> subtree.
		</p>
		<p>
			That matters: it means the total of what is displayed always reconciles with the true total, so a
			capped map never quietly under-reports. Zooming into such an item reveals the levels below it.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="colour">
		<h2 class="doc-section">Colour modes</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><code>Category</code> — a built-in categorical palette keyed off <code>CategorySelector</code>. Hashing is deterministic, so a key always gets the same colour, including across a server and client render.</li>
			<li><code>Depth</code> — progressive shades of one hue, emphasising hierarchy over category.</li>
			<li><code>Heat</code> — a sequential scale driven by <code>HeatSelector</code>, letting you show a second metric (age, percentage of quota, cost) independently of size.</li>
			<li><code>Custom</code> — you supply the colours.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			<code>ColourSelector</code> always wins when it returns a non-empty value, whatever
			<code>ColourMode</code> is set to, so you can override individual items without giving up a
			built-in scheme for the rest.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="sizing">
		<h2 class="doc-section">Sizing and the one piece of JavaScript</h2>
		<p>
			Squarification optimises rectangle aspect ratios, so it has to know the aspect ratio of the area it
			is filling. When <code>Width</code> and <code>Height</code> are not both supplied, a small
			<code>ResizeObserver</code> module reports the container size and the map re-lays-out when it
			changes. Set both parameters and no observer is attached at all.
		</p>
		<p class="text-muted small">
			Give the containing element a height. A tree map fills its container, and a container with no
			height gives it nothing to fill.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="labels">
		<h2 class="doc-section">Labels</h2>
		<p>
			Labels are HTML positioned over the SVG rather than SVG text, so the browser ellipsises them
			properly rather than the component guessing at glyph widths. They ignore pointer events, so clicks
			pass through to the rectangle beneath. Rectangles smaller than <code>MinLabelPx</code> carry no
			label, which is what stops a dense map turning into unreadable noise.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="events">
		<h2 class="doc-section">Events</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><code>Click</code> and <code>DoubleClick</code> — the item interacted with.</li>
			<li><code>SelectionChanged</code> — raised after the selection moves; read <code>Selection</code>.</li>
			<li><code>ZoomRoot</code> / <code>ZoomRootChanged</code> — two-way bindable, so a paired table can re-root itself in step with the map.</li>
			<li><code>BeforeZoomChange</code> — cancellable; set <code>Cancel</code> on the arguments to refuse a zoom.</li>
			<li><code>ExceptionHandler</code> — raised if a selector throws, rather than tearing down the render.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

</div>
